From b47f65c3cf67fb12d29b911a21d6502450edd111 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:31:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Kernels: tangent operator assembled directly in the sparse
 matrix

The stiffness matrices computed by kernels are added directly to the
local sparse matrix of the tangent operator, without being stored
element by element: one thread per pair of nodes of an element computes
an N x N block, searches the position of each entry in its row and adds
it atomically (batch::addStiffnessMatrices overload taking a
batch::AssembledStiffnessMatrix, missing entries reported). The pattern
of the matrix is the one built by the first assembly of MFEM.
GetGradient then ends as in MFEM (block diagonal and PtAP in parallel,
RAP in serial), the essential degrees of freedom being eliminated in
place by EliminateBC, on the device if hypre uses it, instead of
EliminateRowsCols, which copies the matrix on the host and computes the
eliminated part (122 ms per call on the GPU for 111843 unknowns).

Review fixes:
- the failures of the computations by kernels in Mult and GetGradient
  are made collective before the following collective operations, so
  that all the processes leave the resolution together;
- the options of the quadrature interpolator are restored even if an
  exception is thrown;
- the vector of the inner forces computed by kernels is kept between
  the evaluations of the residual;
- uniform Dirichlet boundary conditions without degrees of freedom on a
  process no longer invalidate the copy of the unknowns on the device;
- the unneeded reads of the unknowns on the host are removed; the read
  of the essential degrees of freedom is kept, with the debug device of
  MFEM any access on the device protecting the copy on the host.

GradientsKernelsTest compares the assembled matrix with the assembly by
MFEM and checks the detection of entries outside of the pattern.
